after 16 days of non-stop negotiations  on a possible nuclear deal with Iran US  Secretary of State John Kerry was  hopeful that a solution could soon be  reached but he said difficult questions  still had to be answered including to  what extent inspectors would have access  to Iran's military sites and how quickly  economic and financial sanctions would  be lifted the West has long suspected  Iran of using its nuclear research  program for military purposes that's an  accusation Tehran vehemently denies it  insists that has the right to use  nuclear power to generate electricity  the new deal stipulates that Iran freeze  its research program in response  economic sanctions which have crippled  the country in recent decades would be  loosened even if a deal is reached in  Vienna there's no certainty it would be  ratified israel says it will call on the  u.s. to block the proposals and US  president barack obama must win approval  from congress before any deal goes  through high-ranking republicans in  washington are already saying they'll  vote no it would be the most significant  improvement in relations between the  west and Iran since the Islamic  Revolution in 1979 that something Obama  will be keen to achieve as his term as  US president draws to a close 1201
 

